blication date: December 2025.\\n'}, {'page': 12, 'text': '1:12   •   Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\\n\\n\\n\\n\\nFig. 4. Chain of Though Prompting. In blue at the top the prompt, in green at the bottom the answer. When shown the\\nlonger example prompt—the chain of thought—the LLM follows the steps when answering the question [Wei et al., 2022b].\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n                    Fig. 5. Comparison of PDDL and natural language for Blocksworld [Bohnet et al., 2024]\\n\\n\\nthe problem specification). LLaMEA (Large Language Model Evolutionary Algorithm) leverages evolutionary\\ncomputation methods to generate and optimize evolutionary algorithms [van Stein and Bäck, 2024].\\n  Planners are also combined with LLMs at the language level. Bohnet et al. [2024] provide a benchmark for\\nPDDL [Aeronautiques et al., 1998] based planning problems. They study how LLMs can achieve success in the\\nplanning domain (Figure 5).\\n  In Section 2.3 on retrieval augmentation, we will see further approaches where deep learning and symbolic\\napproaches are successfully combined [Gao et al., 2023b].\\n2.1.3 Search Tree. Chain of Thought uses a prompt that causes the model to perform a sequence of steps. When\\nthere is a single next step, that will be taken. When there are more possibilities, it is unclear how the next step\\n\\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n'}], 'evidence_ids': ['ev-0aaa960f62d9cf1689f1', 'ev-5ee42fcee0f3b22d5203'], 'delivery': 'Originals queued for the next model request; large batches may span requests.'} has no attribute finditer"}], "tool_calls": null, "raw": null, "token_usage": null}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Model-authored note overview, sources=['paper.pdf']:\noverview 最终笔记（含 p.30–31 更新）：三类能力为 reason、act、interact（p.1 摘要；p.3 定义\"Agents that receive input in natural language from their environment, reason to make decisions, and take autonomous actions\"）；第2节 reasoning（reasoning、reflection、retrieval 改进决策）、第3节 acting（tools 与机器人集成为 assistant）、第4节 interacting（multi-agent 交互与 agent 社会）（p.3）。文献组织：先用 Google Scholar 检索 Agentic LLM，再用 snowballing 扩展被引但未收录的工作，仅选 LLM-based 方法（p.5）。第3.4.1 节讨论 AI Scientist、AI Co-Scientist、SWE-Agent 与 MLGym：MLGym 未发现模型能生成新颖假设或算法（p.30–31）；作者指出 agent 尚不能独立产出突破性科学成果，但已带来文献分析、想法生成、实验设置与写作等实际生产力提升（p.31）。答案：categories=reason/act/interact；search_methods=google_scholar, snowballing。"}], "tool_calls": null, "raw": null, "token_usage": null}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Model-authored note tool_use, sources=['paper.pdf']:\ntool_use 笔记：Toolformer（Schick et al., 2023）训练模型自行决定调用哪个 API、何时调用、传什么参数、如何把结果并入后续 token 预测；测试的五种工具为 calculator、question_answering、search_engine、translation、calendar（p.23：\"A range of tools is tested: a calculator, a question-answering system, a search engine, a translation system, and a calendar.\"）。\nToolBench（Qin et al., 2023）收录 16,464 个来自 RapidAPI 的公开 REST API（p.23：\"ToolBench [Qin et al., 2023] contains 16,464 APIs from RapidAPI, a large dataset of publicly available REST APIs.\"）。对比：ToolAlpaca 3938 instances/400 APIs；EasyTool 基于 ToolBench 做结构化工具文档；Gorilla/APIBench 生成 API 调用。\n答案：toolformer_tools=[calculator, question_answering, search_engine, translation, calendar]；toolbench_api_count=16464。Evidence: page 23 (ev-731fa63b8d34dde45e62)."}], "tool_calls": null, "raw": null, "token_usage": null}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Model-authored note multi_agent, sources=['paper.pdf']:\n第4节三步：LLM 社会能力；角色配对/小团队；无预设角色的开放式社会（p.32）。CAMEL 用 inception prompting 与 role descriptions 让两个 LLM 保持预定义角色协作，无需人工持续提示（p.37，Fig.22）。Generative Agents：25 个 agent 的小镇，perceive/remember/reflect/retrieve/plan（p.39–41）；可信度由 100 名人类评估者按四类排序，完整架构最佳（p.47）。更大规模：AgentSociety 超 10,000 agents；AgentVerse 四阶段；OASIS 最多百万 agent，基于 CAMEL（p.40–42）。答案：camel_prompting=inception_prompting；generative_agents_evaluators=100。"}], "tool_calls": null, "raw": null, "token_usage": null}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Model-authored note reasoning, sources=['paper.pdf']:\nreasoning 笔记（增量）：第2节把推理方法分为 prompting（CoT）、search tree、self-reflection、neurosymbolic 等。CoT 贪婪地只走看起来最好的一步、丢弃其它选项；Tree of Thoughts（Yao et al., 2024）建立外部控制算法，用生成 prompt + 评估 prompt + 外部搜索算法系统地树形探索推理步骤，回溯后尝试替代路径，论文给出两种控制器（p.13）：\"When one reasoning path has been traversed, the search backtracks, and tries an alternative. The paper describes both a breadth-first and a depth-first controller.\" 同页：\"A greedy method selects the single step that looks best, follows only that step, and forgets the alternatives (Chain of Thought).\" 故 tot_search=[breadth_first, depth_first]。Self Consistency 的具体选答规则（多条路径结果如何确定最终答案）仍待第12页内容补齐。"}], "tool_calls": null, "raw": null, "token_usage": null}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Model-authored note outlook, sources=['paper.pdf']:\noutlook 笔记（p.51–52）：Table 4 列出五个研究议题——Training Data（用推理期数据微调、稳定强化学习、VLA 与多模态）、Hallucination（self verification、mechanistic interpretability、开放世界模型探索）、Agent Behavior（可扩展仿真与角色扮演、蒸馏到小模型、agent 与人类行为建模）、Self reflection（模型内自我反思与元推理、元认知与人格、自动化科学发现）、Safety（责任与义务、隐私与数据公平、assistant 更广应用领域）(p.51)。结论：三类能力互补，reasoning 是基础，机器人交互与工具使用建立在 grounded retrieval augmentation 上，社会交互建立在 self reflection 上，\"all categories benefit from reasoning and self-verification.\" (p.52) 答案：agenda_topics=[training_data, hallucination, agent_behavior, self_reflection, safety]；shared_foundations=[reasoning, self_verification]。"}], "tool_calls": null, "raw": null, "token_usage": null}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Evidence ev-5ee42fcee0f3b22d5203 | paper.pdf | page:12 | sha256=b4767423c79db69ae7138f4c0aed7733af6385e0a370da21ad7d6fe029d7fb8c\n1:12   •   Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\n\n\n\n\nFig. 4. Chain of Though Prompting. In blue at the top the prompt, in green at the bottom the answer. When shown the\nlonger example prompt—the chain of thought—the LLM follows the steps when answering the question [Wei et al., 2022b].\n\n\n\n\n                    Fig. 5. Comparison of PDDL and natural language for Blocksworld [Bohnet et al., 2024]\n\n\nthe problem specification). LLaMEA (Large Language Model Evolutionary Algorithm) leverages evolutionary\ncomputation methods to generate and optimize evolutionary algorithms [van Stein and Bäck, 2024].\n  Planners are also combined with LLMs at the language level. Bohnet et al. [2024] provide a benchmark for\nPDDL [Aeronautiques et al., 1998] based planning problems. They study how LLMs can achieve success in the\nplanning domain (Figure 5).\n  In Section 2.3 on retrieval augmentation, we will see further approaches where deep learning and symbolic\napproaches are successfully combined [Gao et al., 2023b].\n2.1.3 Search Tree. Chain of Thought uses a prompt that causes the model to perform a sequence of steps. When\nthere is a single next step, that will be taken. When there are more possibilities, it is unclear how the next step\n\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n"}], "tool_calls": null, "raw": null, "token_usage": null}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Evidence ev-99f0f0a37d328bfed1a9 | paper.pdf | page:51 | sha256=b4767423c79db69ae7138f4c0aed7733af6385e0a370da21ad7d6fe029d7fb8c\n                                                                                                               Agentic LLM     •   1:51\n\n\n         Topic               Challenge\n         Training Data       Finetune with inference time reasoning data\n                             Convergent/stable reinforcement learning\n                             VLA, Multimodal signals, such as speech\n         Hallucination       Use Self Verification\n                             Use Mechanistic Interpretability\n                             Use Open Ended/Open World Models for exploration\n         Agent Behavior      Scalable simulation infrastructure, role playing\n                             Distill reasoning to small models\n                             Models of agent and human behavior, emergent behavior, future of society\n         Self reflection     In-model self reflection and metareasoning\n                             Metacognition, personality\n                             Automated Scientific Discovery\n         Safety              Assistants: Responsibility, liability\n                             Privacy, fairness of data\n                             Wider application areas for assistants\n                                Table 4. Summary of Research Agenda for Agentic LLM\n\n\n\n\n  When we add outside state to the input prompts, the input to the LLM will differ based on the history, and so\nwill the answers of the LLM. Differences in memory may be preceived as a personality of the LLMs by its users.\nThe question if LLMs with outside memory exhibit a personality is a topic for future research.\n  Self reflective methods are being used to create agents to perform scientific discovery [Eger et al., 2025]. How\nthese agents will influence, and possibly improve, the process of scientific discovery is an exciting area of research.\n\n   Safety. Safety is a crucial issue in LLMs that act in the world. The problem is studied, but far from being solved\n[Brunke et al., 2022, Andriushchenko et al., 2025, Samvelyan et al., 2024]. Actions by assistants and robots in the\nreal world have real world consequences. When a financial trading assistant hallucinates, or when a self driving\nrobot makes a wrong inference, questions on responsibility and liability should be addressed. More legal and\nethical questions arise, for example, on privacy and fairness, and, possibly, concerning the rights of algorithmic\nentities [Harris and Anthis, 2021, Bengio and Elmoznino, 2025].\n   The application areas for the assistants in this survey—shopping, medical diagnosis, finance—are narrow. The\nnarrower the application domain, the better the answers.\n   Ensuring the safety of agentic LLMs requires moving beyond prompt-based defenses toward integrated, multi-\nlayer safeguards. Key directions include embedding explicit safety constraints within the agent’s reasoning\nand planning pipeline and employing continual adversarial training and automated red-teaming to enhance\nrobustness against manipulation. Further priorities are developing mechanisms for self-regulation and risk\nawareness, enabling agents to detect and avoid unsafe actions, and establishing rigorous, standardized safety\nbenchmarks such as AgentHarm [Andriushchenko et al., 2025]. Together, these measures outline a roadmap for\naccountable and trustworthy deployment of agentic LLMs in high-stakes domains.\n   Clearly, many safety, ethics and trust issues will have to be addressed before the full breadth of the possibilities\nof agentic LLMs can be enjoyed. Safety and ethics will become important topics on the research agenda of agentic\nLLMs, deserving their own surveys and books [Gan et al., 2024, Jiao et al., 2025, Raza et al., 2025].\n\n                                       Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n"}], "tool_calls": null, "raw": null, "token_usage": null}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Evidence ev-4049593a30e2846f8394 | paper.pdf | page:13 | sha256=b4767423c79db69ae7138f4c0aed7733af6385e0a370da21ad7d6fe029d7fb8c\n                                                                                                               Agentic LLM     •   1:13\n\n\n\n\n       Fig. 6. Reasoning structure of Chain-of-Thought, Self-Consistency, and Tree-of-Thoughts [Yao et al., 2024]\n\n\nshould be selected. A greedy method selects the single step that looks best, follows only that step, and forgets the\nalternatives (Chain of Thought). Ideally, we should follow the tree of all possible steps. This method is chosen in\nthe Tree of Thoughts approach [Yao et al., 2024]. Here, an external control algorithm is created, that calls the\nmodel, each time with a different prompt, so that it follows a tree of reasoning steps. When one reasoning path\nhas been traversed, the search backtracks, and tries an alternative. The paper describes both a breadth-first and a\ndepth-first controller.\n   Together, the trio that consists of a generation prompt, an evaluation prompt, and an external search algorithm,\nallows a systematic tree-shaped exploration of the space of reasoning steps. Figure 6 illustrates the different\nreasoning structures. (Another approach, Graph of Thoughts, allows even more complex relations between the\nreasoning steps [Besta et al., 2024].)\n   Many works introduce variants on external prompt-improvement loops, to have explicit control over the\nreasoning process. They use techniques from planning and tree search [Hart et al., 1968, Plaat, 1996] to be able to\nuse backtracking to traverse the space of possible combinations of reasoning steps [Yao et al., 2024, Xie et al.,\n2024, Besta et al., 2024, Schultz et al., 2024, Browne et al., 2012, Gandhi et al., 2024]. Other methods are also\nused for prompt creation. Evolutionary algorithms [Romera-Paredes et al., 2024, van Stein and Bäck, 2024] and\nplanning methods [Bohnet et al., 2024, Valmeekam et al., 2023, Kambhampati et al., 2024] are used to create new\nprompts and heuristic algorithms for LLMs, and, synergistically, to use LLMs to create new heuristic evolutionary\nand planning algorithms.\n   The external search algorithm can also be used to generate training data, for finetuning the LLM, or for\npretraining. In this way, we can try to see if an LLM can be taught to search possible steps implicitly, without\nthe need for an external control loop. In the Stream of Search approach Gandhi et al. [2024] create a language\nfor search sequences, and subsequently train an LLM on search trees that contain both good and bad outcomes,\nimproving the accuracy of the model. This approach internalizes the outcome of external searches into the\nLLM. Schultz et al. [2024] further show how such search results can be used to train an LLM and achieve\nGrandmaster-level performance in Chess, Connect Four, and Hex.\n\n2.2   Self Reflection\nReasoning methods draw inspiration from step-by-step human solution approaches. The more elaborate ap-\nproaches use explicit planning-like methods to look ahead and use feedback for verification. These methods use a\n\n                                       Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n"}], "tool_calls": null, "raw": null, "token_usage": null}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Not fully delivered yet: ev-01ce8948bcc88d235ea3. Continue the pending batch; do not reread unchanged sources. Write notes only from originals already received."}], "tool_calls": null, "raw": null, "token_usage": null}], "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_37", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "python_interpreter", "arguments": "r = read_pdf(11, 12)\nfor p in r['pages']:\n    t = p['text']\n    low = t.lower()\n    hits = [i for i in range(len(low)) if low.startswith('consisten', i) or low.startswith('majority', i)]\n    print('=== PAGE', p['page'], 'len', len(t), 'hits', hits[:10])\n    for i in hits[:2]:\n        print('>>>', t[max(0, i-1400):i+900])\n        print()"}}], "error": null, "model_output_message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "re 模块在这段代码中不可用；我改用逐字扫描，只打印第 11–12 页中与 Self-Consistency 选择规则相关的上下文。\n\n```python\nr = read_pdf(11, 12)\nfor p in r['pages']:\n    t = p['text']\n    low = t.lower()\n    hits = [i for i in range(len(low)) if low.startswith('consisten', i) or low.startswith('majority', i)]\n    print('=== PAGE', p['page'], 'len', len(t), 'hits', hits[:10])\n    for i in hits[:2]:\n        print('>>>', t[max(0, i-1400):i+900])\n        print()\n```", "tool_calls": null, "raw": {"_type": "ChatCompletion", "id": "fd49e04c-bd74-40e2-bfde-3af687725ff0", "choices": [{"_type": "Choice", "finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "logprobs": null, "message": {"_type": "ChatCompletionMessage", "content": "re 模块在这段代码中不可用；我改用逐字扫描，只打印第 11–12 页中与 Self-Consistency 选择规则相关的上下文。\n\n```python\nr = read_pdf(11, 12)\nfor p in r['pages']:\n    t = p['text']\n    low = t.lower()\n    hits = [i for i in range(len(low)) if low.startswith('consisten', i) or low.startswith('majority', i)]\n    print('=== PAGE', p['page'], 'len', len(t), 'hits', hits[:10])\n    for i in hits[:2]:\n        print('>>>', t[max(0, i-1400):i+900])\n        print()\n```", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null}}], "created": 1793334909, "model": "deepseek-flash", "object": "chat.completion", "moderation": null, "service_tier": null, "system_fingerprint": "bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6", "usage": {"_type": "CompletionUsage", "completion_tokens": 1350, "prompt_tokens": 9098, "total_tokens": 10448, "completion_tokens_details": {"_type": "CompletionTokensDetails", "accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 1189, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"_type": "PromptTokensDetails", "audio_tokens": null, "cache_write_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 4224}}, "_request_id": null}, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 9098, "output_tokens": 1350, "total_tokens": 10448}}, "model_output": "re 模块在这段代码中不可用；我改用逐字扫描，只打印第 11–12 页中与 Self-Consistency 选择规则相关的上下文。\n\n```python\nr = read_pdf(11, 12)\nfor p in r['pages']:\n    t = p['text']\n    low = t.lower()\n    hits = [i for i in range(len(low)) if low.startswith('consisten', i) or low.startswith('majority', i)]\n    print('=== PAGE', p['page'], 'len', len(t), 'hits', hits[:10])\n    for i in hits[:2]:\n        print('>>>', t[max(0, i-1400):i+900])\n        print()\n```", "code_action": "r = read_pd